<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The app's colour palette.

    These are raw colour values only. They are never referenced from a layout directly — themes.xml
    maps them onto Material 3 *role* attributes (colorPrimary, colorSurface and so on), and layouts
    use those roles via ?attr/. That indirection is what makes dark mode work: the roles stay the
    same, and only the values behind them change.

    **The scheme.** PetCare is built on warm cream and white with a single bright lemon-yellow
    accent. The reasoning behind each decision:

     - *Cream, not white, as the page.* A pure #FFFFFF background makes every white card on it
       invisible. Backing the app with a warm cream (#FFFBF3) and giving cards true white lets the
       cards lift off the page through colour alone, before a single pixel of shadow is drawn.
     - *One accent, and only one.* [accent_yellow] is the app's single accent, and it is the *same*
       value in light and dark mode rather than a pair — see the note on that colour for why this
       one role breaks the light/dark pattern every other colour here follows. Yellow reads as
       friendly and animal-adjacent rather than clinical, and nothing else in the app competes with
       it, so wherever it appears, that is the thing to press. It is a *light* colour, which is why
       colorOnPrimary is near-black: white text on this yellow would fail contrast badly.
     - *Pastel stat pills.* A separate family of soft tinted chips (see the pill section below) used
       for small facts — a pet's age, weight, species. They are deliberately low-saturation so a row
       of them reads as information rather than as five competing buttons.

    **Naming.** Each name states its scheme and role: md_theme_light_primaryContainer is the
    "primary container" role in the light scheme. The pairing matters — every "on" colour is the
    one guaranteed to be legible on top of the role it names, so text placed on colorPrimary must
    use colorOnPrimary and nothing else.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- ================================ The accent ================================
         The one bright colour in the app, and the only colour here that is deliberately *identical*
         in light and dark mode.

         Every other value in this file comes as a light/dark pair, because a colour that works on a
         cream page rarely works on a near-black one. The accent is the exception on purpose: it is
         never a background for body text and never a page — it is always a *fill* with near-black
         content on top of it (a button, the selected tab's pill, a pet card's status pill). That
         relationship is unchanged by the theme around it, so splitting it into two values would
         make the brand shift shade between modes for no gain.

         It is referenced by both values/themes.xml and values-night/themes.xml, which is what makes
         "one accent throughout" a fact about the code rather than a convention to remember. -->
    <color name="accent_yellow">#F5E050</color>

    <!-- The content colour guaranteed legible on the accent. Near-black, not white: the accent is a
         light colour and white on it measures around 1.5:1, far below the 4.5:1 text needs. -->
    <color name="on_accent_yellow">#1C1A05</color>

    <!-- The ground the opening animation is played on — see the overlay in activity_main.xml and
         Theme.PetCare.Splash in themes.xml.

         It is the app's own cream page colour, the same #FFFBF3 as md_theme_light_background below.
         Written out as its own value rather than pointed at that one because the two answer
         different questions: that colour is "what is the page behind the app's content", this one is
         "what is behind the opening animation", and the day the splash artwork changes only one of
         them should move.

         **It does two jobs, and the first is the one that matters most.** The system paints a
         placeholder window before a single line of the app has run, and whatever colour that is, is
         the first thing the user ever sees. Theme.PetCare.Splash pins it to this value, so the window
         opens in the same cream the overlay then holds: nothing flashes, because nothing changes.

         That holds while the system is in its light theme, which is the case this was asked to fix.
         In dark mode the device repaints the launch window near-black regardless of what the app
         asks for, and the app cannot decline it — the measurements and the two things tried are
         recorded on Theme.PetCare.Splash in themes.xml, so nobody has to rediscover them.

         **The second job is the moment before the first frame.** The overlay holds this colour from
         the instant it is laid out until the decoder produces a picture, so it is what bridges the
         launch window and the video. The two being the same colour is what makes that handover
         nothing to look at.

         It is deliberately *not* the animation's own ground, which is a bright yellow. Matching the
         artwork would matter if the video were letterboxed, because then this would be the colour of
         the bands around it — but the clip is scaled to *cover* the screen precisely so that no band
         exists at any screen shape (see MainActivity.fillVideoToView), which leaves this colour
         showing only before playback and behind the closing fade. At both of those moments the app's
         own cream is the right answer and yellow would be a flash of something else.

         Deliberately *not* themed, either. In the dark theme the app underneath is near-black, but
         the animation is a light one, and opening a light window on a dark theme for two seconds is
         far less jarring than a near-black frame arriving before a bright yellow video. -->

    <!-- A note on what is *not* here: the animation's own ground, #FDE227. Nothing needs it while the
         video covers the screen, and a colour nothing reads is a colour that goes stale. If the clip
         is ever letterboxed again, it belongs back here. -->
    <color name="splash_ground">#FFFBF3</color>

    <!-- ============================== Light scheme ============================== -->

    <!-- Primary: the accent. Carries the main actions — the FAB, filled buttons, the selected
         bottom-navigation tab. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_primary">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onPrimary">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>
    <!-- The muted variant, used behind content that should read as *related* to the accent without
         shouting: highlighted chips, the tint behind a category icon. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_primaryContainer">#FBF2C4</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onPrimaryContainer">#2A2604</color>

    <!-- Secondary: a warm taupe, for supporting elements that should not compete with primary. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_secondary">#6F6350</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onSecondary">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_secondaryContainer">#F7E9D2</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onSecondaryContainer">#261A04</color>

    <!-- Tertiary: sage green, the health accent. Used sparingly for contrast against the yellow. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_tertiary">#4F6A50</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onTertiary">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_tertiaryContainer">#D3EDD1</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onTertiaryContainer">#0C2010</color>

    <!-- Error: reserved for destructive actions and validation failures. Kept close to the
         Material default red, because a warning that looks like the brand stops reading as a
         warning. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_error">#BA1A1A</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onError">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_errorContainer">#FFDAD6</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onErrorContainer">#410002</color>

    <!-- Neutrals — the heart of the redesign.

         background is the warm cream the whole app is laid on; surface is the true white the cards
         are drawn in. They are deliberately *different* here, where before they were the same
         value: that single point of separation is what lets a card read as a card. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_background">#FFFBF3</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onBackground">#1F1B13</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_surface">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onSurface">#1F1B13</color>
    <!-- The quieter surface, for a card that should recede behind the one next to it. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_surfaceVariant">#F3EADB</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_onSurfaceVariant">#6B6152</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_outline">#9C9182</color>
    <!-- Hairline dividers and card strokes. Very close to the background on purpose: the card's
         shadow does the separating, and a visible border on top of it would look drawn twice. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_outlineVariant">#E8DECB</color>

    <!-- Inverse roles: used by Snackbars, which sit on a surface opposite to the rest of the app
         so they read as temporary and layered above it. -->
    <color name="md_theme_light_inverseSurface">#34302A</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_inverseOnSurface">#F8EFE2</color>
    <color name="md_theme_light_inversePrimary">#F0DB6A</color>

    <!-- =============================== Dark scheme ===============================
         Not simply the light scheme inverted. In dark mode the cream page becomes a near-black and
         the white cards become a *lighter* charcoal, keeping the same relationship: the card is
         still the lighter of the two, so it still lifts off the page. The role pairings are
         unchanged, which is exactly why layouts need no dark-mode variants of their own.

         The accent is the one thing that does not change between the schemes — see [accent_yellow]
         above. -->

    <color name="md_theme_dark_primary">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onPrimary">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_primaryContainer">#4A4308</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onPrimaryContainer">#FBF2C4</color>

    <color name="md_theme_dark_secondary">#DCCBB2</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onSecondary">#3C3425</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_secondaryContainer">#544A3A</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onSecondaryContainer">#F7E9D2</color>

    <color name="md_theme_dark_tertiary">#B7D0B5</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onTertiary">#223725</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_tertiaryContainer">#384E3A</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onTertiaryContainer">#D3EDD1</color>

    <color name="md_theme_dark_error">#FFB4AB</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onError">#690005</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_errorContainer">#93000A</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onErrorContainer">#FFDAD6</color>

    <!-- The dark equivalent of the cream-page / white-card split above.

         Pulled down to a genuine near-black for the redesign. The dashboard's pet cards are dark in
         *both* themes — a photo needs a dark ground under its scrim whatever the surrounding page is
         doing, see the pet-card section below — and against the warmer, lighter charcoal this scheme
         used before, those cards read as barely distinguishable from the page they sit on. A
         near-black page restores the separation without touching the cards. -->
    <color name="md_theme_dark_background">#0E0F0A</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onBackground">#E8E6DA</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_surface">#1B1C14</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onSurface">#E8E6DA</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_surfaceVariant">#26271C</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_onSurfaceVariant">#C4C2B2</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_outline">#8F8D7D</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_outlineVariant">#333426</color>

    <color name="md_theme_dark_inverseSurface">#E8E6DA</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_inverseOnSurface">#2F3026</color>
    <color name="md_theme_dark_inversePrimary">#6E6410</color>

    <!-- =============================== Accent text ===============================
         The colour for text and icons that should read as *accented* — a task's time of day, an
         expense's amount, a category heading.

         These cannot simply use colorPrimary, and the reason is the whole point of the redesign's
         palette. The accent is a bright yellow, chosen because it is light and cheerful; light is
         exactly what text on a white card must not be. #FFC53D on white is roughly 1.6:1, far below
         the 4.5:1 that ordinary text needs, and a user would be squinting at every amount in the
         app.

         So the accent has two forms: the bright yellow that *fills* things — buttons, the selected
         tab, the floating action button, all of which carry dark text on top — and this deeper olive
         that *is* text. Same hue, so they read as the same accent; very different lightness, so both
         are legible in the role they play. In dark mode the problem reverses and the yellow itself
         becomes the legible choice, which is why the dark value below is the accent again.

         Exposed to layouts as ?attr/colorAccentText — see attrs.xml. -->
    <color name="accent_text_light">#6B5C00</color>
    <color name="accent_text_dark">@color/accent_yellow</color>

    <!-- ============================ Selected container ============================
         The fill for a chosen day, a chosen filter chip and the Delegate button on the Tasks tab.
         Exposed as ?attr/colorSelectedContainer — see attrs.xml for why the light and dark values
         are not simply a lighter and darker version of each other. -->
    <color name="selected_container_light">#17180F</color>
    <color name="on_selected_container_light">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_selected_container_neutral_light">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="selected_container_dark">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_selected_container_dark">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_selected_container_neutral_dark">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>

    <!-- The resting (disabled) partner of the selected container, for the sign-in screens' main
         button. Light: the charcoal and pale cream from the auth mockups. Dark: a charcoal a step above
         the near-black page, with the theme's muted text. See attrs.xml. -->
    <color name="disabled_container_light">#3A3830</color>
    <color name="on_disabled_container_light">#ECE9DE</color>
    <color name="disabled_container_dark">#34352A</color>
    <color name="on_disabled_container_dark">#9A998A</color>

    <!-- ============================== Pastel stat pills ==============================
         The soft tinted chips that carry a single small fact — a pet's age, its weight, how many
         tasks are left today.

         These sit outside the Material role system deliberately. The roles answer "how important is
         this?", and every pill is equally unimportant; what a pill needs instead is a colour that
         *distinguishes* it from the pill beside it, so a row of three facts can be told apart at a
         glance without reading the labels. Four hues, each with the text colour guaranteed legible
         on it, exposed to layouts as theme attributes — see attrs.xml and themes.xml.

         Light-mode pills are pale washes with dark text. Dark-mode pills invert that relationship:
         a deep, desaturated ground with pale text. Keeping the same *hue* across both is what makes
         "the mint one is age" stay true in either theme. -->

    <!-- Mint — used for age. -->
    <color name="pill_light_mint">#D9F2E4</color>
    <color name="pill_light_on_mint">#0F3D28</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_mint">#1E3A2C</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_on_mint">#B5E6CB</color>

    <!-- Sky — used for weight. -->
    <color name="pill_light_sky">#D9E9F7</color>
    <color name="pill_light_on_sky">#123247</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_sky">#1D3344</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_on_sky">#B3D7EF</color>

    <!-- Lilac — used for species. -->
    <color name="pill_light_lilac">#E6DFF7</color>
    <color name="pill_light_on_lilac">#2C2246</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_lilac">#2F2A43</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_on_lilac">#CFC2F0</color>

    <!-- Peach — used for outstanding care, where a little warmth is wanted without alarm. -->
    <color name="pill_light_peach">#FBE3D6</color>
    <color name="pill_light_on_peach">#4A2513</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_peach">#43291C</color>
    <color name="pill_dark_on_peach">#F5C9B0</color>

    <!-- ========================== Dashboard pet cards ==========================
         The dashboard's pet cards carry a photograph with a dark gradient scrim over the lower half,
         and the pet's name written across it.

         **These are single values rather than light/dark pairs, and that is the point.** Everything
         else in this file is themed, because a colour sitting behind body text has to change with
         the page. These colours sit on top of a *photograph*, which does not change with the theme —
         a white name over a dark scrim is legible over any photo in either mode, and swapping to a
         dark name in light mode would make it disappear into the picture behind it. The card is
         therefore dark in both themes by design, and reads as a photographic object on the page
         rather than as another sheet of paper.

         The same logic covers the card with no photo yet: the placeholder ground is dark too, so a
         pet gaining a photo does not change the card's character. -->

    <!-- The card's ground, seen where a photo has not been added and behind the scrim. -->
    <color name="pet_card_ground">#17180F</color>

    <!-- The scrim laid over the photo's lower portion so the name stays readable on a light or busy
         picture. Drawn as a gradient between these two — see drawable/bg_pet_card_scrim.xml.

         The transparent end has to be the *same* RGB as the opaque end with a zero alpha channel.
         Fading to #00000000 instead would pass through grey on the way, which shows as a dirty band
         across the middle of every photo. -->
    <color name="pet_card_scrim_transparent">#0017180F</color>
    <color name="pet_card_scrim_opaque">#E617180F</color>

    <!-- Text drawn on the card: the pet's name and the chips' labels. -->
    <color name="on_pet_card">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!-- The dark capsule holding a pet's age and weight, and the species chip in the card's top
         corner. Semi-transparent, so a little of the photo shows through and they read as sitting
         *on* the picture rather than as opaque tiles punched out of it. -->
    <color name="pet_card_chip">#7A0E0F0A</color>

    <!-- ========================== Pet detail: photo card ==========================
         The pet detail screen's large photo card follows the pet-card rules above — dark in both
         themes, white on a scrim — and adds three marks of its own on top of the photograph. They are
         single values rather than light/dark pairs for the same reason: the photo behind them does not
         change with the theme. -->

    <!-- The round back button in the photo's top-left corner: a near-white disc with the dark arrow
         on it. Light rather than the translucent dark chip, because it is the one control on the
         photo and has to be found instantly, over a dark scrim-free patch of picture or a pale one. -->
    <color name="pet_detail_back_button">#F2EFE3</color>
    <color name="on_pet_detail_back_button">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>

    <!-- The gender symbol beside the species chip. Blue for male and pink for female, the
         conventions people already read these symbols in, so the colour confirms the shape rather than
         asking to be learned. Both sit on the translucent dark chip (pet_card_chip), so they are the
         lighter, brighter end of each hue: a mid blue would sink into the dark chip. -->
    <color name="gender_male">#6CB4FF</color>
    <color name="gender_female">#FF8AC4</color>

    <!-- ========================= Bottom navigation bar =========================
         The bar is a floating control: a translucent, hairline-bordered slab hovering over whatever
         page is scrolling beneath it, with the current tab widened into a filled pill.

         **These are light/dark pairs, where the old dark capsule was a single treatment.** The bar
         is no longer an opaque object with a world of its own inside it — it is a *surface*, and a
         surface that lets the page show through has to belong to the page's theme. A near-white slab
         over the near-black night page would glare; a near-black slab over the cream day page would
         read as a solid bar again, which is the thing this design is not.

         They are still declared here rather than taken from the Material roles, because the roles
         carry no alpha and the pill's fill has no role at all: "the strongest possible mark on this
         page" flips from near-black to yellow between the themes. Each pair is bound to a theme
         attribute in values/themes.xml and values-night/themes.xml — see attrs.xml. -->

    <!-- The slab itself: white at 86% in the day theme, near-black at 90% at night. The alpha is
         what makes the bar read as floating *over* content rather than as a band cut out of it — a
         hint of the page's colour comes through and moves as the page scrolls. -->
    <color name="nav_bar_surface_light">#DBFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="nav_bar_surface_dark">#E6141412</color>

    <!-- The active tab's pill, and the icon and label written on it. The pairs are inverses of each
         other: near-black pill with yellow on it by day, yellow pill with near-black on it at night.
         Either way the active tab is the highest-contrast thing on the bar, which is the property
         that has to survive the theme — a near-black pill on the night page would disappear into it. -->
    <color name="nav_tab_active_light">#14130F</color>
    <color name="nav_tab_active_dark">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_nav_tab_active_light">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_nav_tab_active_dark">#14130F</color>

    <!-- The four inactive tabs' icons.

         **Solid values, deliberately, rather than the on-surface ink at 70% alpha.** They are the
         same colour to the eye, but not the same thing: an alpha would let the scrolling page show
         through the glyphs themselves, so a tab icon would change shade as a dark photo passed
         underneath it — and a translucent icon over a translucent bar compounds to something too
         faint to read. These are the blend already done, measured against the bar's own tone: about
         6:1 against the day slab and about 8:1 against the night one, so they stay legible while
         staying clearly quieter than the active tab. -->
    <color name="nav_tab_muted_light">#625F57</color>
    <color name="nav_tab_muted_dark">#A8A79E</color>

    <!-- The Tasks tab's open-count badge while that tab is *inactive*: a red dot with white on it,
         the one place in the bar the accent is not used. It is not a "selected" mark but an alert,
         and the accent already means "you are here" two tabs away — the same colour saying both
         would make the count read as a second active tab. Single values: a warning keeps its colour
         in either theme. -->
    <color name="nav_badge_ground">#FF4D2E</color>
    <color name="on_nav_badge">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!-- The same badge once the Tasks tab *is* active. Red on the near-black pill is legible but
         reads as an error inside the control the user has just pressed, so on the pill the badge
         drops to the pill's own inverse — accent on near-black by day, near-black on accent at
         night — and is simply a number the user is already looking at. -->
    <color name="nav_badge_active_light">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_nav_badge_active_light">#14130F</color>
    <color name="nav_badge_active_dark">#14130F</color>
    <color name="on_nav_badge_active_dark">@color/accent_yellow</color>

    <!-- ============================== Places tab ==============================
         The app's shared dark-panel pair, and the "open now" pill.

         **Single values rather than light/dark pairs,** for the same reason as the pet cards and the
         bottom bar above: these colour panels that are dark in *both* themes. They are named for the
         Places tab because its distance strip was the first thing to use them; that strip has since
         been replaced by the map card, but the two values below are now the app's standard dark-card
         ground and its muted ink on it, borrowed by the Expenses total, the Home "next up" card, the
         Profile card and the locked-out sign-in screen — which is why they stay. -->

    <!-- The dark-panel ground — the same near-black as the bottom bar, so every dark element on a
         page reads as one family. -->
    <color name="places_strip_ground">#17180F</color>
    <!-- Small labels on that ground: quiet but legible, measuring about 6:1. -->
    <color name="places_strip_muted">#9A998A</color>
    <!-- The "OPEN NOW" pill on the featured place. Green, because open/closed is a status and green is
         what "open" looks like on every shop door and maps listing. A fill with near-black text, like
         the accent, so it works unchanged on a white card and a charcoal one. -->
    <color name="open_now_container">#34C77B</color>
    <color name="on_open_now">#062B17</color>

    <!-- ================================= Money tab =================================
         The dark total card, and the colour that stands for each spending category.

         **The card is dark in both themes**, for the reason given for the Places strip above: it is a
         small chart, and coloured segments read most clearly on a dark ground. Its colours are the
         strip's, referenced rather than repeated, so the two dark cards stay one family. -->
    <color name="total_card_ground">@color/places_strip_ground</color>
    <color name="total_card_amount">#FFFFFF</color>
    <!-- The empty bar, before a month has any spending: a step lighter than the card, just visible,
         and nothing like a category colour. -->
    <color name="total_card_track">#33352A</color>

    <!-- **Category colours.** Single values rather than light/dark pairs: each colour is drawn as a bar
         segment on the dark card *and* as a dot on the page, and the page changes with the theme while
         the card does not. They were therefore picked mid-light — saturated enough to stand out on
         the near-black card, dark enough that a 10dp dot still shows on the cream page — and far enough
         apart in hue that neighbouring segments never blur together.

         Medication takes the accent, Food a warm grey and Grooming green, as in the design; the rest
         continue round the colour wheel. -->
    <color name="expense_category_medication">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="expense_category_food">#A39D8A</color>
    <color name="expense_category_grooming">#34C77B</color>
    <color name="expense_category_vet">#4F93F0</color>
    <color name="expense_category_toys">#FF8457</color>
    <color name="expense_category_accessories">#AE86EE</color>
    <color name="expense_category_insurance">#26AFA6</color>
    <color name="expense_category_training">#EC6AA2</color>
    <color name="expense_category_other">#76725F</color>

    <!-- ============================== Home dashboard ==============================
         The redesigned Home tab: the dark "next up" card, the yellow insight card, the header's unread
         dot, the sync dot and the coloured bars in "coming up this week".

         **The two feature cards do not follow the theme,** for the reason given for the pet cards and
         the Money tab's total card above: each is a single deliberate treatment. The next-up card is the
         one dark panel on the page in either mode, so the eye lands on "what to do now" first; the
         insight card is the one yellow panel, the accent used as a fill with near-black content on it,
         exactly as the accent is used everywhere else. Their colours are therefore fixed values, and
         several are *references* to existing ones so the dark cards across the app stay one family. -->

    <!-- The next-up card's ground, title, supporting text and ring track: the Places strip and Money
         card values, referenced rather than repeated. -->
    <color name="next_up_card_ground">@color/places_strip_ground</color>
    <color name="next_up_card_title">@color/total_card_amount</color>
    <color name="next_up_card_muted">@color/places_strip_muted</color>
    <color name="next_up_ring_track">@color/total_card_track</color>

    <!-- The insight card: the accent as the ground, its guaranteed-legible dark for the sentence, and a
         softened olive for the small "TODAY'S INSIGHT" label (about 5:1 on the yellow, enough for a
         caption while staying visibly quieter than the sentence). The "AI" badge is the dark card
         ground, with the accent written on it. -->
    <color name="insight_card_ground">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_insight_card">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_insight_card_muted">#5A5314</color>
    <color name="insight_badge_ground">@color/pet_card_ground</color>

    <!-- The dot on the header badge when care is overdue. A clear red, the colour every app uses for
         "something needs you", in both themes: it shows against the cream page and the near-black one
         alike. -->
    <color name="header_unread_dot">#FF4D3D</color>

    <!-- The dot beside "All synced". Green when everything is backed up (the "open now" green, so the
         app has one green for "all good"), and the accent while changes are still waiting. The offline
         case uses the theme's outline colour, set in code. -->
    <color name="sync_dot_synced">@color/open_now_container</color>
    <color name="sync_dot_pending">@color/accent_yellow</color>

    <!-- **Care category colours**, for the thin bar on each "coming up this week" row. Single values
         rather than light/dark pairs: a 3dp bar on a white or charcoal card needs a mid-light,
         saturated colour to show either way, like the spending category colours above.

         Grooming takes the accent, vet visits red and medication blue, as in the design; the rest reuse
         the spending palette's hues so a colour means roughly the same thing on both tabs. -->
    <color name="care_category_grooming">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="care_category_vet">#E5484D</color>
    <color name="care_category_medication">#4F93F0</color>
    <color name="care_category_walk">#34C77B</color>
    <color name="care_category_feeding">#A39D8A</color>
    <color name="care_category_training">#EC6AA2</color>
    <color name="care_category_play">#FF8457</color>
    <color name="care_category_other">#76725F</color>

    <!-- ================================ Profile screen ================================
         The large dark header card carrying the photo, the name and the two pills, and the pale green
         "VERIFIED" pill on the account rows.

         **The header card does not follow the theme**, for the reason given for the pet cards, the
         Places strip and the Money tab's total card above: it is a single deliberate treatment — the
         one dark panel on the page in either mode, so the eye lands on "this is you" first — and it
         carries a *photograph*, which does not change with the theme. Its values are therefore fixed,
         and several are references to existing ones so the dark panels across the app stay one family. -->

    <!-- The card's ground and the text on it: the Places strip and Money card values, referenced
         rather than repeated. -->
    <color name="profile_card_ground">@color/places_strip_ground</color>
    <color name="on_profile_card">@color/total_card_amount</color>
    <!-- The email and phone lines under the name. Quiet against the card but comfortably legible,
         measuring about 6:1 on the ground above. -->
    <color name="on_profile_card_muted">@color/places_strip_muted</color>

    <!-- The circular photo well. A step lighter than the card so an empty well reads as a space for a
         picture, with a faint rim marking its edge where the two are close. -->
    <color name="profile_photo_well">#24261A</color>
    <color name="profile_photo_well_stroke">#3A3C2C</color>

    <!-- The dark pet-count pill beside the yellow role pill. The translucent chip the pet cards use,
         so a dark pill on a dark panel is the same object here as it is on a photograph. -->
    <color name="profile_count_pill">@color/pet_card_chip</color>

    <!-- The "VERIFIED" pill on the email and phone rows. A pale green wash with dark green text: green
         because verified is a *status* and green is what "confirmed" looks like, pale because these
         pills are read and never pressed — a saturated fill would make four quiet rows look like four
         buttons. It is a pastel stat pill in everything but name, so it follows those rules (see the
         pill section above) and comes as a light/dark pair. -->
    <color name="verified_pill_light">#DCF2E1</color>
    <color name="on_verified_pill_light">#14401F</color>
    <color name="verified_pill_dark">#1C3624</color>
    <color name="on_verified_pill_dark">#B9E5C6</color>

    <!-- ===================== Log in: wrong-password warnings and the lock =====================
         The yellow card after a wrong password, the red one on the last attempt before the lock, and
         the dark panel that replaces the form once sign-in is locked.

         **The two alert cards come as light/dark pairs** (reached through the roles in attrs.xml),
         unlike the dark panels elsewhere in the app: these sit *inline in the form*, between the
         password field and the button, so they have to belong to the page around them in either
         theme. A fixed pale yellow wash would glare on the near-black page, and a fixed dark one
         would look like a hole punched in the light one.

         The **badge fills and the lock red are single values**, for the reason the pet cards and the
         bottom bar are: an exclamation mark in a coloured square and a countdown ring on a dark panel
         are marks rather than page content, and their contrast comes from the colour they are drawn
         on, which does not change with the theme. -->

    <!-- The yellow warning card, attempts 1–3: a wash a shade warmer than the page with a visible
         edge, so it reads as an inserted notice rather than as another field. -->
    <color name="auth_alert_warning_fill_light">#FCF6D8</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_warning_edge_light">#E3D06A</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_warning_fill_dark">#2E2A11</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_warning_edge_dark">#5C5320</color>

    <!-- The red card on the last attempt. The same construction in the error hue, so the two cards
         are plainly the same object with the volume turned up rather than two different notices. -->
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_fill_light">#FDECE8</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_edge_light">#EFB3A6</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_fill_dark">#33201C</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_edge_dark">#6B3A31</color>

    <!-- The red card's bold title. Not colorError: that red is tuned for a thin line of text on the
         page, and on this pale red wash it sits too close to the fill. These are a step darker in
         light mode and a step lighter in dark, measuring comfortably over 4.5:1 on the fill above. -->
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_title_light">#B03A22</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_title_dark">#FFB4A0</color>

    <!-- The exclamation badge on each card. The warning's is the app's accent with its guaranteed
         legible near-black on it, exactly as the accent is used everywhere else; the danger's is the
         lock red below, with white on it. -->
    <color name="auth_alert_warning_badge">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_auth_alert_warning_badge">@color/on_accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="auth_alert_danger_badge">@color/auth_lock_red</color>
    <color name="on_auth_alert_danger_badge">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!-- The one red the lockout is drawn in: the countdown ring, the "ACCOUNT LOCKED" label and the
         danger badge above. A warm red rather than the Material error red, which would read as a
         validation failure; this is a state the account is in, not a mistake in a field. Light enough
         to hold up against the near-black panel it is drawn on. -->
    <color name="auth_lock_red">#E5664A</color>

    <!-- The locked panel. Dark in both themes for the reason given for the dashboard's next-up card
         and the profile header above — it is a single deliberate treatment, and the one dark panel on
         the page either way, so the eye lands on it before the two info rows beneath. Its values are
         the existing dark-card family, referenced rather than repeated, so all the app's dark panels
         stay one family. -->
    <color name="auth_lock_card_ground">@color/places_strip_ground</color>
    <color name="on_auth_lock_card">@color/total_card_amount</color>
    <color name="on_auth_lock_card_muted">@color/places_strip_muted</color>
    <!-- The unfilled remainder of the countdown ring: a step lighter than the panel, just visible,
         and nothing like the red that fills it. -->
    <color name="auth_lock_ring_track">@color/total_card_track</color>

    <!-- A satisfied rule in the password checklist (ui/common/PasswordChecklistView.kt).

         The "open now" green, referenced rather than repeated, because the app deliberately has one
         green meaning "all good" — the same one behind the Places tab's OPEN NOW pill and the
         dashboard's "All synced" dot. A single value rather than a light/dark pair, for the reason
         the other accents are single: it is a small mark on a page, saturated enough to read against
         the cream and the near-black alike.

         The *unmet* rows take no colour of their own. They use the theme's muted text colour, which
         is exactly what they are — text that has not become important yet. -->
    <color name="password_rule_met">@color/open_now_container</color>

    <!-- ============================== "Add a place" screen ==============================
         The sub-screen reached from the Places tab, where a place is saved from the device's GPS,
         and the map card the Places tab itself now leads with.

         Two groups, split by the same rule the rest of this file follows. The first are light/dark
         *pairs* exposed through theme attributes (see attrs.xml), because they sit on the page and
         must follow it. The second are single values, because the things they colour sit on a map or
         on a dark panel and look the same in either theme — exactly the reasoning behind the bottom
         bar above. -->

    <!-- The ground a map card shows *while it loads*, on both the Places tab and this screen. A pale
         sage by day and a deep slate by night: the map is a surface being drawn on, so it follows the
         theme — a bright panel on the near-black page would be the loudest thing in the app, and a
         white flash before the tiles arrive is exactly what this colour exists to prevent. Used by
         the card the map sits in; see PlaceMapView. -->
    <color name="map_ground_light">#DFE5D8</color>
    <color name="map_ground_dark">#15191A</color>

    <!-- The "you are here" dot: on the map placeholder, on the current-location card, and in the
         status chip over the live map.

         Blue in both themes, and the same blue the Places tab has always used for the same reason: a
         blue dot in a halo is the universal "you are here" mark, and recognition is worth more than
         palette purity. A white ring separates it from whichever ground it lands on. -->
    <color name="here_dot">#2E7DFF</color>
    <color name="here_dot_ring">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!-- The status chip's dot when there is *no* fix — permission refused, or the attempt failed. A
         warm coral rather than a red: this is a condition to resolve, not an error the user caused,
         and the screen still works through the demo location beside it. -->
    <color name="gps_status_unavailable">#FF8A6B</color>

    <!-- The translucent near-black behind anything laid *over* a map — the Places tab's name pill and
         the Add a place screen's status chip — with its pale ink. Fixed in both themes rather than
         following the page: these sit on Google's tiles, not on the app's surface, and a light pill
         would vanish into a map that is pale by day and inverted-pale by night.

         One value for both, at about 80%: translucent enough that the map reads through it as ground,
         opaque enough that the tiles behind never compete with the text on it. -->
    <color name="map_overlay_ground">#CC14130F</color>
    <color name="on_map_overlay">#F5F2E8</color>

    <!-- The map's secondary action ("GOOGLE MAPS"), a solid near-black capsule beside the accent
         "DIRECTIONS". Fixed in both themes for the same reason as the overlays above. -->
    <color name="map_action_secondary">#14130F</color>
    <color name="on_map_action_secondary">#F5F2E8</color>

    <!-- The current-location card. Dark in both themes: it is a single strong statement — "this is
         where you are" — and a white card would make it one more field in the form. The muted ink is
         a solid value rather than the light ink at reduced opacity, which is the rule this screen
         follows throughout. -->
    <color name="gps_card_ground">#14130F</color>
    <color name="on_gps_card">#F5F2E8</color>
    <color name="on_gps_card_muted">#C7F5F2E8</color>
    <!-- The outline on the card's "Refresh location" pill — the card's own ink, softened, so the
         quiet action reads as secondary to the accent "Use demo location" that can sit beside it. -->
    <color name="gps_card_button_stroke">#66F5F2E8</color>
    <!-- The tinted well the blue dot sits in, on the card and on the map placeholder: the same blue
         at a little under a fifth. -->
    <color name="gps_card_dot_well">#2E2E7DFF</color>

    <!-- The pin that hangs over the middle of the map while it is being dragged to choose a point (see
         ic_map_pin.xml). The accent for the balloon and the app's near-black for its outline and hole,
         fixed in both themes for the same reason as the overlays above: it sits on Google's tiles
         rather than on the app's surface, and a pin that followed the page would vanish into a map
         that is pale by day and inverted-pale by night. -->
    <color name="map_pin_fill">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="map_pin_ink">#14130F</color>

    <!-- ========================= Vaccination history =========================
         The status pill at the end of each row on a pet's vaccination card, and the thin bar down its
         left-hand edge. Three states — clear (green), overdue (red), due soon (the accent) — worked
         out from the due date every time the card is drawn; see ui/pet/VaccinationStatus.kt.

         The split between what is a light/dark *pair* and what is a single value is the same rule the
         rest of this file follows, and it falls differently for the two marks:

          - **The pills are pairs.** A pill is a filled panel with text on it, sitting on a white card
            by day and a charcoal one at night. A pale mint wash with dark green text is right on the
            first and glaring on the second, so each state has a wash-with-dark-ink value for the light
            theme and a deep-ground-with-pale-ink value for the dark one — exactly the relationship the
            pastel stat pills above use. The hue is held constant across both, which is what makes
            "the red one needs attention" stay true in either theme.
          - **The bars are single values.** A 3dp capsule carries no text and is read purely as a
            colour, so it needs a saturated mid-light value that shows against the cream page and the
            near-black one alike — the same reasoning as the care-category bars above. -->

    <!-- Clear: a one-off dose that never repeats, and a booster comfortably in the future. Green,
         because the app already has one green that means "all good" (the OPEN NOW pill, the
         "All synced" dot, a satisfied password rule) and this is another reading of it. -->
    <color name="vaccination_clear_light">#E4F5EA</color>
    <color name="on_vaccination_clear_light">#0B5133</color>
    <color name="vaccination_clear_dark">#1E2B23</color>
    <color name="on_vaccination_clear_dark">#8FE3B8</color>

    <!-- Overdue: the due date has passed. Red, and the only red on the card — which is the point. It
         is deliberately not colorError: that red is tuned for a line of validation text on the page,
         and this is a filled pill saying a real-world appointment has been missed. -->
    <color name="vaccination_overdue_light">#FDEAE5</color>
    <color name="on_vaccination_overdue_light">#B7301A</color>
    <color name="vaccination_overdue_dark">#2B1E1A</color>
    <color name="on_vaccination_overdue_dark">#FF8A6B</color>

    <!-- Due today, or due inside the next month.

         **The one pair here that is identical in both themes**, and for the reason given for the
         accent itself at the top of this file: it is the app's accent used exactly as the accent is
         always used — a bright fill with near-black content on it — and that relationship does not
         change with the page behind it. The ink is a touch deeper than on_accent_yellow, matching the
         near-black the design uses wherever the accent fills a small control. -->
    <color name="vaccination_due">@color/accent_yellow</color>
    <color name="on_vaccination_due">#14130F</color>

    <!-- The bars. Brighter and more saturated than the pills they sit opposite, because a 3dp sliver
         needs more colour than a filled panel to register at all. -->
    <color name="vaccination_bar_clear">#2BD98A</color>
    <color name="vaccination_bar_overdue">#FF6B4A</color>
    <color name="vaccination_bar_due">@color/accent_yellow</color>

</resources>
